#host("Farid Responds", ("We as Muslims, we believe in the divine command theory. The idea that those things which are deemed to be right and those things which are deemed to be wrong are right and wrong because God said or ordered that they are right and wrong. It's simple as that. And the way we prove this is by proving that God exists and that God has absolute and complete knowledge which which can be argued for through philosophical arguments and through historical proofs of the Quran's preservation of the Quran's miraculous language and etc. indirectly proving God and proving his messenger which is our main source of morality.", "So essentially what I'm saying is the burden of proof is upon you not us. When you criticize Islam on moral grounds you have to give us a standard by which we're going to measure morality. You can't just come and say X is false because I feel that X is false or X is false because X harms people. That's using the harm principle. And although we are against harm, the prophet sallallaihi wasallam forbade harm, something that you think harms people might not be harming people. My question to is actually how do you prove that equality is moral in the first place?", "It is a burden of proof upon you to show us why equality should be taken as a moral standard because we as theists not just Muslims but but as theists can simply say that anything that is included in inequality or so-called what you call equality if it opposes God's will then it is immoral and many people will agree with me regardless if they're Muslim or not. So the main argumentation and the main debates should not be on these singular separate points. They should be on whether what we deem to be revealed by God is actually from God.", "Does God exist? And if he does, does he reveal this? If he does reveal this, then it follows that this is the moral grounding. So that is where the discussion should go. It shouldn't go in the direction of what about X point, what about Y point